[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Added the remaining lint-legal positive-path contract scenarios: negated and ordering wildcard comparators (!=1.x outside the 1.x band, >1.x bumping to >=2.0.0, >=1.x), hyphen ranges whose endpoints are wildcards (1.x - 2.x => >=1.0.0 <3.0.0; 1.2.x - 1.4.x => >=1.2.0 <1.5.0), and prerelease precedence where numeric identifiers rank below string identifiers (1.0.0-2 < 1.0.0-alpha) and longer identifier sequences rank higher when the common prefix matches (1.0.0-alpha < 1.0.0-alpha.1), asserted through checked compare_precedence, plus these constructs added to the accepted-range grammar scenarios. On the contracted *Error outcomes: I verified with the deterministic oracle (bin/check_files) that ANY test or transition that asserts or even names a *Error is rejected with NO_ERROR_TESTING: 'Contract violation errors (*Error) designate checked preconditions that callers must satisfy and cannot be tested ... Preconditions cannot be tested, even if explicitly checked in code. Tests and transitions must never assert or expect *Error exceptions.' The companion guide mandates the same rule (checked preconditions are gated via require/require_arg and never asserted). Therefore InvalidVersionError/InvalidRangeError/PackageNotFoundError/ResolutionConflictError are unassertable in this role by enforced rule; they remain modeled as caller-precondition domains on the interface transitions, and their exception-type verification belongs to the downstream verification suite that low/semver_solver.pyi refers to ('Verification suites MUST assert on exception types'), not to pretense scenario tests.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/lib/semver_solver_impl.py: lib/semver_solver_impl.py: _expand_wildcard_token at lines 121-138 expands every ordering comparator applied to a wildcard into a single inequality against the wildcard's inclusive lower bound (line 138), so the range >1.x compiles to >1.0.0 and admits versions that lie inside the 1.x band; the VersionEngine.satisfies and VersionEngine.parse_range contracts in low/semver_solver.pyi and low/semver_solver_impl.pyi require wildcard tokens to normalize into paired comparator inequalities that preserve the band's exclusive upper boundary, and they explicitly permit relational operators on wildcard targets, so a strict greater-than comparison against the 1.x band must evaluate True for 2.0.0 and False for 1.9.9 and 1.0.0. The failing sequence parse_range('>1.x') followed by satisfies(parse_version('1.9.9'), range, False) currently returns True, contradicting the contracted wildcard semantics; the same lower-bound-only dispatch also cannot express the upper-bound boundary required for less-than-or-equal and greater-than wildcard comparators, so ordering comparators on wildcards are not normalized correctly in general. The latest CHANGE only replaced the optional operator lookup with the total _ordering_operator helper and pruned unreachable raises; it did not correct how ordering comparators select the wildcard band boundary, so this contract discrepancy remains.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/lib/semver_solver_impl.py: Correct wildcard ordering-comparator normalization in _expand_wildcard_token: bare/caret/tilde wildcards expand to the inclusive band [lower, upper), '>' now starts at the band's exclusive upper boundary (so >1.x compiles to >=2.0.0 and rejects 1.9.9/1.0.0), '<=' ends at the exclusive upper boundary, '>=' uses the inclusive lower boundary, '<' uses the lower boundary, and '!=' complements the band. Added the absolute minimum version for empty-band cases and made the any-version wildcard cover all versions.
